---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { load } from 'js-yaml';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import InfoPage from '../components/InfoPage.astro';
// For teachers. Every number is read at build time from what the lesson map is built from (content/map.yaml and each
// lesson's minutes), so it cannot drift. The other facts are the About, Help, Privacy, Terms and Licences pages' and the
// Settings dialog's own; the finish page and save code facts are src/pages/done/[...slug].astro and src/progress/code.ts.
const order = (load(readFileSync('content/map.yaml', 'utf8')) as { id: string }[]).map((p) => p.id);
const pillars = (await getCollection('map')).sort((a, b) => order.indexOf(a.id) - order.indexOf(b.id)).map((p) => p.data);
const minutes = new Map((await getCollection('lessons')).map((l) => [l.id, l.data.minutes]));
const counts = pillars.map((p) => p.topics.flatMap((t) => t.lessons.map((slug) => minutes.get(`${p.id}/${slug}`)!)));
const range = (key: 'simple' | 'deep') => {
  const all = counts.flat().map((m) => m[key]);
  return `from ${Math.min(...all)} to ${Math.max(...all)} minutes`;
};
const lessons = (n: number) => `${n} ${n === 1 ? 'lesson' : 'lessons'}`;
const toc = [
  { id: 'what', title: 'What Stillpool is' },
  { id: 'needs', title: 'What a class needs' },
  { id: 'pillars', title: 'The pillars' },
  { id: 'shape', title: 'How a lesson is shaped' },
  { id: 'ways', title: 'Ways to use it with a group' },
  { id: 'badge', title: 'What the badge means' },
  { id: 'access', title: 'Access' },
  { id: 'licence', title: 'Licence' },
  { id: 'contact', title: 'Contact' },
];
---

<Base title="For teachers" description="How to use Stillpool, the free AI lessons by NeuroBridgeEDU, with a class or a group: what it needs, how a lesson runs and what a badge means." wide>
  <InfoPage title="For teachers" {toc}>
    <p slot="intro" class="lede">This page is for teachers and trainers who want to use Stillpool with a group.</p>

    <h2 id="what">What Stillpool is</h2>
    <p>
      Stillpool is a free site for learning about AI, with no adverts, no account and no cookies. It puts neurodivergent learners first, with
      short lessons on calm pages. No lesson is timed or scored.
    </p>

    <h2 id="needs">What a class needs</h2>
    <p>
      A class needs a web browser. There is nothing to install and no sign-up. Stillpool asks for no name, email or account. Progress and
      Settings need JavaScript to be turned on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each learner's progress and settings stay in their own browser and are not sent anywhere. The host, Vercel, receives ordinary request
      details such as an IP address, as for any web page. The <a href="/privacy/">Privacy notice</a> has the full list.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pillars">The pillars</h2>
    <p>The lessons sit in {pillars.length} pillars. There are {lessons(counts.flat().length)} in all. Choose a pillar to see it on the lesson map.</p>
    <ul>
      {
        pillars.map((p, i) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/#pillar-${p.id}`}>{p.title}</a>: {lessons(counts[i].length)}. Topics: {p.topics.map((t) => t.title).join('; ')}.
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2 id="shape">How a lesson is shaped</h2>
    <p>
      Each part of a lesson is written twice. Simple says the idea in short, plain words. Deep adds the detail and the examples. Simple always
      shows, and a switch adds Deep.
    </p>
    <p>
      Near the end is a check-in, a few short questions. Every answer comes with an explanation. A learner can try again as often as they like,
      and nothing is scored. Every lesson lists its sources.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each lesson states an approximate time. Across all lessons, it runs {range('simple')} with Simple only. With the Deep layers it runs {range('deep')}.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ways">Ways to use it with a group</h2>
    <p>These are suggestions. Change them to fit your group.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One lesson as homework, then a discussion.</strong> Learners read a lesson and do its check-in on their own. The group then talks about it in class.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>One lesson together on a projector.</strong> Read the lesson as a group and do the check-in together. Text size goes up to Largest.
        Focus mode hides the header navigation links, the breadcrumb, the contents list and the footer. Settings stays. A badge earned on the projector stays in
        that browser only.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Learners choose a lesson from one pillar.</strong> Every lesson is open and none has to come first. Each learner picks one that interests them.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="badge">What the badge means</h2>
    <p>
      A badge is earned by doing a lesson's activity: the check-in questions near the end. Finishing a lesson on its own does not earn one. A
      badge is not a score, and Stillpool does not lead to a qualification.
    </p>
    <p>
      A badge is kept in the learner's own browser. There is no account, so a teacher cannot look up a learner's badges. There is no teacher view.
    </p>
    <p>
      A learner can show you the finish page on their own device. It says "Badge earned" when that device holds the badge. A learner can also show
      their save code. It holds the lessons they finished and the badges they earned, and nothing else.
    </p>
    <p>
      To read a code, restore it in a browser. The lesson map then shows what it holds. Restore adds to the progress already in that browser.
    </p>

    <h2 id="access">Access</h2>
    <p>The Settings button is in the header on every page. Changes apply as you make them and stay in the browser.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Read aloud:</strong> a recorded voice or a voice on the device, at Normal speed or up to 1.5 times faster.</li>
      <li><strong>Text size:</strong> five steps, from Small to Largest.</li>
      <li><strong>Spacing:</strong> letter and word spacing, and line spacing.</li>
      <li><strong>Fonts:</strong> Lexend, Public Sans, the system font and OpenDyslexic.</li>
      <li><strong>Themes:</strong> Light, Dark, High contrast, or the same as the device.</li>
      <li><strong>Movement:</strong> a Reduce motion switch.</li>
      <li><strong>Focus:</strong> a Reading ruler, and Focus mode. Focus mode hides the Lesson map, Search and Help links, the breadcrumb, the contents list and the footer. Settings stays.</li>
      <li><strong>Text versions:</strong> a switch to show interactives as text.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <a href="/accessibility/">Accessibility statement</a> has the detail and the known limits.</p>

    <h2 id="licence">Licence</h2>
    <p>
      The lessons are under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/" rel="noopener">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>. You may copy, share,
      remix and build on them. You must give credit, link to the licence and say if you changed anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      You may not use them to make money. If you share your own version, it must carry the same licence. You may not suggest that the authors
      back you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The credit to give is: "&lt;lesson title&gt;" by Emanuel Covasa, from Stillpool, licensed under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Add a link to the lesson and a
      note of any changes you made.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site's code is all rights reserved. The NeuroBridgeEDU name and logo are not covered by the lesson licence. The <a href="/licences/">Licences and credits</a> page has the detail.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
    <p>
      To report a mistake in a lesson or a barrier in the site, write to <a href="mailto:contact@neurobridgeedu.eu">contact@neurobridgeedu.eu</a>.
      Please do not email a save code.
    </p>
  </InfoPage>
</Base>
